-- title:
--   Finiteness of admissible Whittaker functions along the GL₃ torus
-- statement:
--   Let $v$ be a finite place of $\mathbb{Q}$ (a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$), and let $\varpi$ lie in the valuation ring of $\mathbb{Q}_v$ with nonzero image in $\mathbb{Q}_v$ and $|\varpi| = \exp(-1)$, so that $\varpi$ is a uniformiser. Let $\eta$ be an additive character of $\mathbb{Q}_v$ with values in $\mathbb{C}$ which is not identically $1$ on $\{x : |x| \le \exp(1)\} = \varpi^{-1}\mathcal{O}_v$, and let $W : GL_3(\mathbb{Q}_v) \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfy $W(u(x,y,z)g) = \eta(x+y)\,W(g)$ for all $x,y,z \in \mathbb{Q}_v$ and all $g$, where $u(x,y,z)$ is the upper triangular unipotent matrix with entries $x$, $y$ above the diagonal and $z$ in the corner. Assume $W$ is right invariant under some open subgroup, and that for every open subgroup $U_v$ there is a finite set $B$ of functions such that every right $U_v$-invariant element of the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the right translates $h \mapsto W(hg)$ of $W$ lies in the span of $B$. Fix $L \in \mathbb{N}$. Write $t(k,m) = \mathrm{diag}(\varpi^{k+m}, \varpi^{m}, 1)$, the image under the upper-left embedding $GL_2 \hookrightarrow GL_3$ of $\mathrm{diag}(\varpi^{k},1)\cdot\mathrm{diag}(\varpi,\varpi)^{m}$. Then there are $d_1, d_2 \in \mathbb{N}$ and coefficients $c_1 : \mathrm{Fin}\,d_1 \to \mathbb{C}$, $c_2 : \mathrm{Fin}\,d_2 \to \mathbb{C}$, independent of the vector below, such that every $F$ in the span of the right translates of $W$ which is right invariant under all $k \in GL_3(\mathbb{Q}_v)$ having $k$ and $k^{-1}$ integral and $|k_{ij} - \delta_{ij}| \le \exp(-L)$ for all $i,j$ satisfies: $F(t(k,m)) = 0$ whenever $k < -L$ or $m < -L$; for all $k, m \ge -L$ the two recursions $F(t(k+d_1,m)) = \sum_{i} c_1(i) F(t(k+i,m))$ and $F(t(k,m+d_2)) = \sum_i c_2(i) F(t(k,m+i))$ hold; and there are reals $C \ge 0$ and $R \ge 1$, depending on $F$, with $\|F(t(k,m))\| \le C\,R^{(k+L) + (m+L)}$ for all $k,m \ge -L$ (the exponent being the sum of the natural-number truncations of $k+L$ and $m+L$).
--
--   This is the finiteness of the restriction of an admissible Whittaker function to the diagonal torus of $GL_3(\mathbb{Q}_v)$, in the form of vanishing off a quadrant, two linear recursions with coefficients uniform over the cyclic module generated by $W$, and a geometric bound. It is the input used to prove convergence of the local Rankin–Selberg integrals against such Whittaker functions, the rationality of the local zeta function as a quotient by a polynomial, and the summation of the local zeta integral over valuation shells.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_LanglandsTunnell_CubicInduction_exists_forall_apply_iotaGL_diagZ_mul_scalarPi_zpow_eq_sum_of_isGL3PsiWhittakerFn.lean

